## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
## 1. Synthesis of COPV 1–6
The flat 2-D $\pi$ -system of COPVs has been found to be useful for dye-sensitized SCs, solid-state organic lasers, molecular wires 8 and energy and electron donors. 9 COPV has a 2-D conjugated system flanked by geminal substituents (e.g., at C5) that forms a 3-D scaffold, preventing intermolecular interactions. To study the feasibility of creating a 3-D homoconjugated donor system,9 we installed four (4- arylamino)phenyl donor groups on both ends of the COPVskeleton and on one of the geminal diphenyl groups (at C5) as in 1–3, expecting that the COPV system and the geminal C5 groups would become homoconjugated in the radical cationic state. Compound 3 has a spiro-linkage mimicking spiroMeOTAD, 2 is an open chain analogue of 3, and 1 is its 4- aminophenyl isomer. For comparison, we also synthesized bis(4-diarylamino)phenyl-COPV 4–6 lacking the electrondonating geminal groups (Scheme 1).
## 2. Physical and optical properties of COPV
Then, a strong interaction between the radical cation of the COPV moiety and the electron-rich geminal groups was identified by differential pulse voltammetry (DPV; Figure 2b).
The electrochemical data in Figure 2b provide evidence for strong interaction between the COPV framework and the electron-rich geminal groups in the radical cation state of 1–3, but not that of 4, 5. The fluorene reference compound 7 shows the first and second oxidation peaks $\mathrm{(Flu}^{1+},\mathrm{\DeltaFlu}^{2+}$ ; Figure 2 top) at $-0.03$ and $0.26\mathrm{V}$ (vs. $\mathrm{Fc}^{\dagger}/\mathrm{Fc}$ couple), respectively. The amino COPV reference compounds 4 and 5 show the corresponding peaks $(\mathrm{PV}^{1+},\mathrm{P\hat{V}}^{2+})$ at $-0.04\mathrm{~V}/0.17\mathrm{~V~}$ and – $0.05\mathrm{~V}/0.17\mathrm{~V~}$ , respectively, and the tetramethyl analogue COPV 6 $(-0.15\mathrm{~V}/0.07\mathrm{~V})$ is slightly easier to oxidize. It is notable that the diaminofluorene-COPV 3 shows the $\mathrm{Flu}^{1+}$ and $\mathrm{Flu}^{2+}$ peaks at 0.14 and $0.33{\mathrm{~V~}}$ , much higher by 0.17 and 0.07 V than those of the fluorene reference 7, indicating that the oxidation of the fluorene moiety becomes much more difficult once the COPV moiety is oxidized $(\mathrm{PV}^{1+})$ . Likewise, oxidation of the 4-aminoaryl groups in 1 and 2 becomes so difficult that they are oxidized only after the second oxidation of the COPV core $(\mathrm{PV}^{2+})$ .
