General procedure for the Buchwald–Hartwig amination. A Schlenk tube, charged with the corresponding substrate bromide (14–19), bis(4-methoxyphenyl)amine (1.5 equiv to $\mathrm{C-Br}$ bond), KOt-Bu (1.2 equiv to amine), $\operatorname{Pd}(\mathrm{dba})_{2}$ or $\mathrm{Pd}_{2}(\mathrm{dba})_{3}$ $5\%$ mol Pd per C–Br bond), and $t–\mathrm{Bu}_{3}\mathrm{P}{\bullet}\mathrm{HBF}_{4}$ (2 equiv to Pd), was evacuated and back-filled with nitrogen three times and dry toluene $25~\mathrm{mL}$ per g substrate bromide). The reaction mixture was stirred at $40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ and then heated at reflux for $48\mathrm{~h~}$ before cooling to room temperature. After removal of solvent under vacuum, the residual was flowed through a short column of silica gel with hexane/DCM (plus $1\%$ triethylamine). Preparative gel permeation chromatography (Tol, refraction index detector) gave the product.
